domain StructuredSemanticAuthoringKernelDesignV1Revision3Review:
  description "Evidence-chain review of exact D11 design candidate revision three."
problem P1:
  |
    Determine whether revision three is complete enough to accept against ADR-0032,
    foundation requirement v3, character requirement v5, and its own closed run and
    request state contracts. Distinguish an acceptance-blocking missing transition
    from implementation detail that can remain in a later slice.
evidence E1:
  |
    ADR-0032 lines 42 through 55 separates provider transport timeout, worker lease,
    semantic progress, and cumulative resource ceilings. It states that a provider
    timeout does not terminate the whole attempt only when one accepted bounded
    recovery remains within all other budgets and fence rules.
evidence E2:
  |
    Foundation v3 F9 lines 242 through 258 permits provider outage or exhausted
    authorized execution budget to end an attempt as failed, requires retained source
    and failure reason, and makes all automatic recovery subordinate to F15 limits.
evidence E3:
  |
    Foundation v3 F15 lines 360 through 373 requires terminal stop, no further provider
    work or late-result application after terminal failure, bounded outstanding work,
    a compact failure receipt, and an explicit new attempt for retry.
evidence E4:
  |
    Revision three lines 523 through 529 records provider_transport_timeout and permits
    another call only when the frozen policy allows one recovery, cumulative admission
    succeeds, the worker fence is current, and the strategy is not a blind replay.
    It does not define the run transition when any of those conditions is false.
evidence E5:
  |
    Revision three lines 596 through 612 declares a closed durable state machine and
    accepts provider completion only while the same fence owns a nonterminal run and
    the request is outstanding. The timeout clause does not state that the timed-out
    request ceases to be outstanding before a recovery request is scheduled.
evidence E6:
  |
    Revision three lines 614 through 626 fully defines the analogous process or worker
    lease loss path: fence, account unknown consumption, append failure evidence, move
    to failed with a recoverable technical outcome and resumption recipe, and reject
    late application. No corresponding provider-timeout terminal or replacement-request
    transition is specified.
evidence E7:
  |
    Revision three lines 728 through 736 requires a parameterized conformance fixture
    for provider timeout with zero and one recovery and late results, but a fixture
    cannot select a missing normative state transition without defining product and
    persistence behavior itself.
premise C1:
  |
    A closed state-machine design must define a legal next state for every admitted
    outcome that affects whether provider work may continue and whether a late result
    may mutate trusted state. Otherwise implementation or tests must invent the
    externally material failure and retry contract.
premise C2:
  |
    Exact timeout values and recovery count may remain undecided under ADR-0032, but
    the behavior for both allowed policy values and for exhausted or inadmissible
    recovery must be defined before implementation authority can rely on the design.
premise U1:
  |
    No runtime execution was performed. Provider convergence, numeric timeout fitness,
    and the exact accepted transport policy remain unknown and are outside this review.
decision D1 based_on P1, E1, E4, C2:
  |
    Revision three correctly removes a whole-attempt elapsed-time termination rule and
    separates the provider and worker policies, so ADR-0032's boundary architecture is
    represented.
decision D2 based_on P1, E4, E5, E6, C1:
  |
    The provider-timeout path is incomplete. With zero recovery, exhausted recovery,
    failed admission, or a stale worker fence, the design defines neither a terminal
    run transition nor the failure receipt and resumption path. When recovery is
    admitted, it also does not close or fence the timed-out request before scheduling
    its replacement, leaving late-result eligibility ambiguous.
decision D3 based_on P1, E2, E3, E7, D2:
  |
    The omission is an acceptance-blocking contract defect rather than downstream DDL
    or DTO detail: F9 and F15 require failure, retention, stop, late-result rejection,
    and explicit new-attempt retry semantics, while the planned fixture cannot
    authoritatively invent them.
decision D4 based_on P1, D1, D3, U1:
  |
    Report REVISE with one P1 finding for exact revision three. Require a provider-
    timeout transition that atomically terminalizes the timed-out request, defines the
    same-run replacement request identity and late-result fence when recovery is
    admitted, and otherwise moves the run to failed with a recoverable technical
    outcome, retained receipt/accounting, and an owner-triggered new-run recipe. Do not
    modify the design or infer implementation, provider, cutover, or deployment
    authority from this review.
